"Figure caption: Schematic illustration of the strategy for delivering RNPs to mitochondria and the protocol for verification of its efficacy. (A) Schematic illustration of conventional methods for mitochondrial delivery of RNPs and of the direct delivery method described here. The conventional methods employ plasmid- or RNA-based strategies to deliver Cas9 and sgRNA separately. In contrast, we delivered RNP to mitochondria directly. (B) Schematic illustration of the mitochondrial delivery of RNPs by MITO-Porter. MITO-Porter modified with STR-R8 is positively charged and may contribute to the cellular uptake of particles and their localization to negatively charged mitochondria. (C) Schematic illustration of the protocol used to verify the efficacy of RNP-MITO-Porter. After target compatibility was confirmed by evaluating its cellular uptake and subcellular localization in LMSF-N-MTFVB cells, we verified its efficacy by evaluating mtDNA cleavage.\n\nFigure caption: Design of sgRNA and evaluation of cleavage activity in vitro. (A) A partial excerpt of the mtDNA sequence from C57BL/6 N-mtFVB/NJ mice. The proto-spacer adjacent motif (PAM) sequence (5′-NGG-3′) (pink highlight) near the target point mutation was selected, and 20 bp upstream was used as the sgRNA binding sequence in the design of sgRNA_Atp8. (B) Protocols used to evaluate the cleavage activity of RNP complexes formed from the sgRNA_Atp8s and Cas9 proteins in vitro (a). Two cleaved artificial gene DNA bands and uncleaved DNA were observed in the DNA samples with 2 and 5 equivalents of RNP. The cleavage ratio increased in an RNP equivalent-dependent manner (b). The uncropped (full-size) gel images used in this experiment, with the edges of the gel visible, are provided in Fig. S3B.\n\nFigure caption: Schematic illustration of the protocol used to prepare RNP-MITO-Porter. The RNP complex was formed by stirring Cas9 proteins and sgRNAs (1st step). The RNP solution mixed with HEPES buffer was used as the aqueous phase (aqueous buffer), and the lipid molecules solution constituting MITO-Porter mixed with ethanol was used as the organic phase (lipid suspension). The organic phase was continuously diluted by stirring the two liquids in the unique flow path of the iLiNP device used to prepare the particles (2nd step). Dialysis with buffer as the outer aqueous phase was then used to obtain RNP-MITO-Porter (3rd step). In subsequent experiments, the RNP-MITO-Porter was prepared at a flow rate ratio of 450/50 (µL/min) with a lipid composition of DOPE/SM/STR-R8 = 9/2/1.1.\n\nFigure caption: Evaluation of mtDNA cleavage activity in isolated mitochondria. (A) Protocol used to evaluate cleavage activity in isolated mitochondria. RNP-MITO-Porter (tRNALeu(UUR)) was incubated with mitochondria isolated from HeLa cells. The degree of cleavage was calculated using a comparative qPCR CT method. (B) Percentage of target cleavage achieved by adding RNP-MITO-Porter, RNP-Lipofectamine, RNP + Empty-MITO-Porter, and Naked RNP. The data are presented as the mean ± SD (n = 3). Significant differences were determined using unrepeated one-way ANOVA followed by Dunnett’s test. **p < 0.01 (vs. Naked RNP).\n\nFigure caption: Verification of mtDNA cleavage in HeLa cells. (A) Subcellular localization of RNP-MITO-Porter (tRNALeu(UUR)) using CLSM. When NBD-labeled RNP-MITO-Porter (green) is colocalized with red-stained mitochondria, yellow signals were observed in the merged image. Scale bars, 20 μm. (B) Protocol for cleavage activity in HeLa cells (a). HeLa cells were transfected with RNP-MITO-Porter (tRNALeu(UUR)) [final concentration of RNP, 10 nM]. Cleavage activity was measured at 1.5, 3, and 6 h after the transfection of RNP-MITO-Porter (tRNALeu(UUR)) (b). The data are presented as the mean ± SD (n = 4). Significant differences were determined using an unrepeated one-way ANOVA, followed by Dunnett’s test. *p < 0.05 (vs. 1.5 h).\n\nFigure caption: Verification of mtDNA cleavage in LMSF-N-MTFVB cells. (A) Subcellular localization of RNP-MITO-Porter (Atp8) using CLSM. When RNP-MITO-Porter labeled with DiI (green) is colocalized with red-stained mitochondria, yellow signals are observed in the merged image. Scale bars, 50 μm. (B) Cell uptake analysis of MITO-Porter using flow cytometry. LMSF-N-MTFVB cells were transfected with RNP-MITO-Porter (Atp8) or Empty-MITO-Porter labeled with DiD. The cell uptake was measured at 1, 3, 6, and 24 h after treatment. The level of cell uptake is expressed as the mean fluorescence intensity. The data are presented as the mean ± SD (n = 3). Significant differences were determined using unrepeated ANOVA followed by Dunnett’s test; *p < 0.05, **p < 0.01 (vs. NT). (C) Protocol for evaluating the relative mtDNA copy number in LMSF-N-MTFVB cells (a). LMSF-N-MTFVB cells were transfected with RNP-MITO-Porter (Atp8) and Naked RNP (Atp8). The final concentration of RNP was 20 nM. The relative copy number was measured 24 h after transfection (b). Data are presented as the mean ± SD (n = 3–6). Significant differences were determined using an unrepeated ANOVA followed by Dunnett’s test *p < 0.05, **p < 0.01 (vs. NT).\n\nTable caption: Physical properties of RNP-MITO-Porter."